+\begin{corollary}[Black-box groups of exponent $p$]
+\label{cor:black-box}
+Fix $c \ge 1$ and assume Theorem~\ref{thm:is-import}. Let $G = \langle g_1,\dots,g_s\rangle$ be a black-box group with unique $\ell$-bit encodings and a coherent multiplication oracle $|x,y,z\rangle \mapsto |x,y,z\oplus xy\rangle$, promised to be a $p$-group of class at most $c < p$ and exponent $p$, with $p$ given. Let $f$ be a coherent hiding function of label width $w$ for an arbitrary $H \le G$, and let $\eps \in (0,1)$ be rational; put $L = \lfloor \ell/\log_2 p\rfloor$. A quantum algorithm returns generators of $H$ with probability at least $1-\eps$. It makes no query to $f$ until it has computed an explicit chart. It always queries $f$ at most $c(L+\tau)\prod_{d=2}^{c}B_d(L)$ times, where $\tau = \lceil\log_p(6/\eps)\rceil$ is the $\tau$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:main} at error $\eps/2$, a bound polynomial in $\ell$ and $\log(1/\eps)$, and independent of $s$; on the event, of probability at least $1-\eps/4$, that the kernel step returns $\ker\phi$ exactly, it queries $f$ at most $Q_{\mathrm q}$ times, the count of Theorem~\ref{thm:main} at error $\eps/2$ for $m = \log_p|G|$ and $g_d = \dim\gamma_d(\log G)$. Its other cost is $\poly_c(s,\ell,w,\log p,\log(1/\eps))$ gates and black-box multiplications.
+\end{corollary}
+
+\begin{proof}
+Put $F = F_{s,c}$, the free nilpotent Lie algebra of class $c$ over $\Fp$ on $x_1,\dots,x_s$, of dimension $d = \sum_{k=1}^c \frac1k\sum_{j\mid k}\mu(j)s^{k/j} \le \sum_{k \le c}s^k$, with its Lyndon basis of brackets. Since $G$ has exponent $p$ and class below $p$, $\log G$ is a Lie algebra over $\Fp$ of class at most $c$, so there is a unique homomorphism $\phi : F \to \log G$ with $x_i \mapsto \log g_i$. Its image is a Lie subalgebra containing every $\log g_i$, so by the subgroup form of the Lazard correspondence $\phi$ is onto, and $K = \ker\phi$ is an ideal with $F/K \cong \log G$. Write $\Phi = \exp\circ\phi$.
+
+\emph{Evaluating $\Phi$.} The Lazard sum and bracket of $\log G$ are computed from group elements by fixed straight-line programs of products and powers $z \mapsto z^{r}$, $r \in \mathbb{Z}[1/c!]$, depending only on $c$ (the inverse Baker--Campbell--Hausdorff formulas~\cite[Lemma~10.7 and \S10.2, pp.~115 and~124]{Khukhro1998}, \cite[\S2]{CicaloDeGraafVaughanLee2012}). For completeness we recall why. In the free nilpotent Lie algebra $\Lambda$ of class $c$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ on $X, Y$, made a group by the BCH product, $X+Y$ and $[X,Y]$ are products of rational powers of group commutators in $X$ and $Y$: the product $X \ast Y$ and the group commutator of $X$ and $Y$ agree with them modulo $\gamma_2(\Lambda)$ and $\gamma_3(\Lambda)$, and once a product of such powers agrees with the target modulo $\gamma_k(\Lambda)$, the discrepancy lies in the central quotient $\gamma_k(\Lambda)/\gamma_{k+1}(\Lambda)$, which is spanned by the group commutators of weight $k$, so further rational powers of these correct it modulo $\gamma_{k+1}(\Lambda)$; after weight $c$ the identities are exact. Their exponents have denominators whose prime factors are at most $c$~\cite[\S10.2, p.~124]{Khukhro1998}, \cite[\S2]{CicaloDeGraafVaughanLee2012}, so they lie in $\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}$, and by the Lazard correspondence the same words compute the Lazard sum and bracket in every $p$-group of class at most $c < p$, with $z^{u/v} = z^{uv^{-1} \bmod p}$ when the exponent is $p$ (see also the appendix on torsion and black-box groups of~\fullv, which gives explicit certified words). Since $z^{-1} = z^{p-1}$, only the multiplication oracle is used. So the images $\Phi(w_j)$ of the Lyndon basis are bracket programs in the $g_i$, and $\Phi(u)$ is the Lazard linear combination $\sum_j u_j\Phi(w_j)$, at $O_c(d\log p)$ multiplications. $\Phi$ is computed coherently and its workspace cleaned up by uncomputation. We assume, as for $U_f$ before Lemma~\ref{lem:hybrid}, that the multiplication oracle acts as some unitary on strings that are not encodings; the identity's encoding is $g_1^p$, and multiplication by a classical constant $h$ is controlled by selecting $\mathrm{enc}(h)$ or $\mathrm{enc}(1)$ into the second register.
+
+\emph{Kernel.} Since $\phi$ is additive and $\exp$ is a bijection, $\Phi(u) = \Phi(v)$ exactly when $u - v \in K$. So $u \mapsto \mathrm{enc}(\Phi(u))$ hides the subgroup $K$ of $(F,+) \cong \Fp^d$, and abelian Fourier sampling finds $K$ with no query to $f$. One sample prepares the uniform superposition over $F$, computes $\mathrm{enc}(\Phi(u))$ into a fresh register and discards that register, which leaves the coset state $\rho_K$ on $F \cong \Fp^d$; by Lemma~\ref{lem:fourier} with $J = K$, Fourier transforming and measuring gives a uniform element of $K^\perp$. Since $p^{\dim K^\perp} = |G| \le 2^\ell$, $\dim K^\perp \le \min(d,L)$, so by the hyperplane count in the proof of Lemma~\ref{lem:fourier}, with $\dim K^\perp$ in place of the ambient dimension, $t = \min(d,L) + \lceil\log_p(8/\eps)\rceil$ samples fail to span $K^\perp$ with probability at most $1/((p-1)p^{\lceil\log_p(8/\eps)\rceil}) \le \eps/8$. The samples use $2dt$ Fourier transforms over $\Fp$, a number fixed in advance; compiling each by Theorem~\ref{thm:uniform-compilation}(iii) with $\eta = \eps/(16dt)$, Lemma~\ref{lem:hybrid} on the canonical subspaces, as in Corollary~\ref{cor:approx}, changes the distribution of the samples by at most $\eps/8$ in total variation. So the kernel step fails with probability at most $\eps/4$. Two public checks follow: that $\Phi(v) = 1$ for a basis of $K'$, which fails whenever $K' \not\subseteq K$, and that $K'$ is an ideal of $F$. If a check fails, or if $d - \dim K' > L$, the algorithm aborts. With exact Fourier transforms every sample lies in $K^\perp$, so $K' \supseteq K$ and the first check detects every sampling failure. A deviation $K' \subsetneq K$ can come only from the approximation error and is not detected; it does not affect correctness: if $K'$ is an ideal, $F/K'$ is a Lie algebra of class at most $c$ over $\Fp$ and $E' = \Phi \circ \iota'$ is a surjective homomorphism onto $G$, where $\iota'$ is the non-pivot coordinate embedding for $K'$, defined as $\iota$ is for $K$ in the next paragraph, so the core still returns a basis whose images generate $H$, at the query count for dimension $d - \dim K' \le L$.
+
+\emph{Chart.} Take the row-reduced basis of $K$ and let $\iota : \Fp^m \to F$ put coordinates on the non-pivot positions, $m = d - \dim K = \log_p|G|$. Projecting brackets of $F$ along $K$ gives the structure constants of $F/K \cong \log G$ in these coordinates, by $\poly(d)$ field operations, and $E(y) = \Phi(\iota y)$ is a group isomorphism from $(\Fp^m,\ast)$ onto $G$, computed from the $m$ precomputed images of the coordinate vectors at $O_c(m\log p)$ multiplications.
+
+\emph{Core.} The chart and $f \circ E$ form an instance of Definition~\ref{def:input-contract}, and $f \circ E$ hides $E^{-1}(H)$. Theorem~\ref{thm:main} at error $\eps/2$ returns a basis of $\log E^{-1}(H)$, with $Q_{\mathrm q}$ queries of $f\circ E$, each one query of $f$ and two coherent evaluations of $E$. The images of the basis under $E$ generate $H$. A union bound over the kernel step ($\eps/4$) and the core ($\eps/2$) gives the error. The costs are polynomial because $d \le c\,s^c$ and $m\log_2 p \le \ell$; they include the bracket table of $F$ in its Lyndon basis, the ideal check ($\dim K' \cdot d$ brackets) and the push-forward of the output basis, each $\poly_c(s,\log p)$. The worst-case query bound holds because every branch that reaches the core has dimension $d - \dim K' \le L$ and $g'_d = \dim\gamma_d(F/K') \le L$, and $B_d$ is monotone.
+\end{proof}
