+\begin{remark}[Scope of Corollary~\ref{cor:black-box}]
+\label{rem:black-box-scope}
+The corollary is a field form of the $e = 1$ chart route in the proof of part~(ii) of the black-box free-Lie pullback proposition of~\fullv, with a row-reduced complement in place of the chart lemma used there, stated with Schedule~1 alone. Like Theorem~\ref{thm:main}, it is conditional only on Theorem~\ref{thm:is-import}; its proof uses only results proved here and the inverse Baker--Campbell--Hausdorff formulas from the literature. Given class below $p$, the exponent-$p$ promise can be tested, since $G$ has exponent $p$ exactly when every $g_i^p = 1$. No encoding of $1$ is given, so the test checks that the elements $e_i = g_i^p$ satisfy $e_ie_i = e_i$ and $e_i = e_1$, which under unique encoding holds exactly when every $e_i$ is $1$ ($O(s\log p)$ multiplications). It makes four restrictions; Corollary~\ref{cor:black-box-pe} below removes the first, and the other three apply to it as well. (i) Exponent $p$: for exponent $p^e > p$, see Corollary~\ref{cor:black-box-pe}. (ii) Unique encoding: the corollary, like~\cite{ImranIvanyos2023}, assumes unique encodings. (iii) $p$-groups: for black-box nilpotent groups of class at most $c$, the composition in~\fullv{} (its all-prime black-box theorem) needs, with Schedule~1 alone, beyond Corollary~\ref{cor:black-box-pe} only a Sylow split by order finding and factoring and the exact algorithm of~\cite[Theorem~1]{ImranIvanyos2023} for the primes $p \le c$; this paper does not treat those steps, and the composition is not claimed here. (iv) Oracle input only: the coset-copy input of Theorem~\ref{thm:main} is not covered, since $E^{-1}$ is never computed.
+
+A finite simulation (\file{theorems/rees_blackbox_end_to_end.py}; Appendix~\ref{app:artifacts}) runs the chart step of the corollary from opaque handles on three small unitriangular matrix groups and on the free two-generator class-three group of exponent $5$ given by hidden coordinates, and then the scaled exact simulator of the core on the acquired chart (with the per-grade arities of~\fullv, not Schedule~1 alone). The Fourier sampling law is computed from the fibres of $\Phi$ in floating point, and the homomorphism check on the chart is exhaustive or sampled as recorded. In every run the subgroup generated by the returned handles equals $H$. It is a finite check and proves nothing for other groups.
+\end{remark}
+
+\begin{corollary}[Black-box groups of exponent $p^e$]
+\label{cor:black-box-pe}
+Fix $c \ge 1$ and assume Theorem~\ref{thm:is-import}. Let $G = \langle g_1,\dots,g_s\rangle$, its encodings, its multiplication oracle, $f$, $w$ and $L$ be as in Corollary~\ref{cor:black-box}, except that $G$ is promised to be a $p$-group of class at most $c < p$ of any exponent, with $p$ given, and let $\eps \in (0,1)$ be rational. Put $\tau' = \lceil\log_p(6L/\eps)\rceil$. A quantum algorithm returns generators of $H$ with probability at least $1-\eps$. It makes no query to $f$ until it has computed an explicit chart. On every branch it makes at most $L$ calls of the algorithm of Theorem~\ref{thm:main} and at most
+\[
+  Q_{\mathrm{worst}} = L\cdot c\,(L+\tau')\prod_{d=2}^{c}B_d(L)
+\]
+queries to $f$, a bound polynomial in $\ell$ and $\log(1/\eps)$, and independent of $s$ and of the exponent. For a finite abelian $p$-group $A$ let $\operatorname{rk}A$ be its number of cyclic factors, and put $b = \log_p|G|$, $n = \operatorname{rk}(\log G)$ and $R = \operatorname{rk}([\log G,\log G])$. On the event, of probability at least $1-\eps/4$, that the kernel step returns $\ker\phi$ exactly, at most $b$ calls are made, and call $j$ queries $f$ at most $c(k_j+\tau_j)\prod_{d=2}^{c}B_d(g_{d,j})$ times, where $k_j \le n$ is the dimension of its input Lie algebra, $g_{d,j} \le \min(k_j,R)$ is the dimension of the $d$th term of its lower central series, and $\tau_j = \lceil\log_p(6b/\eps)\rceil$; in all, at most $b\,c\,(n+\tau_j)\prod_{d=2}^{c}B_d(\min(n,R))$ queries. Its other cost is $\poly_c(s,\ell,w,\log p,\log(1/\eps))$ gates and black-box multiplications. If $G$ has exponent $p$, the algorithm is that of Corollary~\ref{cor:black-box}.
+\end{corollary}
+
+\begin{proof}
+\emph{Lazard rings.} We use the Lazard correspondence for finite $p$-groups of class below $p$~\cite{Lazard1954} (see also~\cite[\S10.2 and Example~10.24, p.~124]{Khukhro1998}). Such a group $G$ is the group $(\log G,\ast)$ of a finite Lie ring $\log G$ of $p$-power order and the same class, with the truncated BCH product, whose denominators are prime to $p$; its subgroups are the Lie subrings, and $g^k = k\log g$ for $k \in \mathbb{Z}$. Conversely, every finite Lie ring of $p$-power order and class at most $c < p$ is a group under $\ast$, and Lie ring homomorphisms are group homomorphisms, since they commute with every Lie polynomial. If $J$ is an ideal of a Lie subring $S$ and $x \in S$, every term of $x \ast j$ other than $x$ and $j$ is a bracket containing $j$, so $x \ast J = x + J$. Hence the quotient map $q : S \to S/J$ is a group homomorphism with kernel $J$, and the left cosets of $J$ in $(S,\ast)$ are its additive cosets. Additive generators of a Lie subring generate it as a group, since the subgroup they generate is a Lie subring containing them. For a finite abelian $p$-group $A$, $\operatorname{rk}A = \dim_{\Fp}A[p] = \dim_{\Fp}A/pA \le \log_p|A|$, so $\operatorname{rk}$ does not increase under subgroups (by the first form) or quotients (by the second). Elements of $A$ generate $A$ exactly when their images span $A/pA$, since a subgroup $B$ with $B + pA = A$ has $A/B = p(A/B)$, hence $B = A$.
+
+\emph{Exponent.} First test whether $g_ig_i = g_i$ for every $i$. With unique encodings this holds exactly when $G = 1$, and then the output is empty. Otherwise let $p^e$ be the largest order of a generator, found by repeated $p$th powers with the same identity test, at most $L$ powers per generator since $p^e \le |G| \le 2^\ell$. It is the exponent of $G$: $\{x \in \log G : p^ex = 0\}$ is an ideal, since $p^e[x,y] = [p^ex,y]$, and it contains every $\log g_i$, so it is $\log G$ by the subgroup form. If $e = 1$, run the algorithm of Corollary~\ref{cor:black-box}, whose bounds are within those stated; assume $e \ge 2$ from now on.
+
+\emph{Pullback and kernel.} Let $F$ be the free nilpotent Lie ring of class $c$ over $\mathbb{Z}/p^e$ on $x_1,\dots,x_s$. It is the free $\mathbb{Z}/p^e$-module on the Lyndon basis $w_1,\dots,w_d$ of Corollary~\ref{cor:black-box}, with integer structure constants. Since $\log G$ has class at most $c$ and $p^e\log G = 0$, there is a unique Lie ring homomorphism $\phi : F \to \log G$ with $x_i \mapsto \log g_i$; it is onto by the subgroup form, and $K = \ker\phi$ is an ideal. Put $\Phi = \exp\circ\phi$. It is evaluated as in the proof of Corollary~\ref{cor:black-box}, whose straight-line programs compute the Lazard sum and bracket in every $p$-group of class at most $c < p$, now with $z^{u/v} = z^{uv^{-1} \bmod p^e}$ and $z^{-1} = z^{p^e-1}$, at $O_c(d\,e\log p)$ multiplications. Since $\phi$ is additive and encodings are unique, $u \mapsto \mathrm{enc}(\Phi(u))$ hides $K$ in $(F,+) \cong (\mathbb{Z}/p^e)^d$. With the Fourier transform over $\mathbb{Z}/p^e$ in each coordinate, the computation in the proof of Lemma~\ref{lem:fourier}, with $\omega = e^{2\pi i/p^e}$, shows that a sample is a uniform element of $K^\perp = \{y : y\cdot u \equiv 0 \pmod{p^e}\ \text{for all } u \in K\}$. As an abelian group $K^\perp$ is the character group of $F/K \cong \log G$, so $\operatorname{rk}K^\perp = n \le \min(d,L)$. The images of uniform samples in $K^\perp/pK^\perp \cong \Fp^n$ are uniform, so by the hyperplane count in the proof of Lemma~\ref{lem:fourier}, $t = \min(d,L) + \lceil\log_p(8/\eps)\rceil$ samples fail to generate $K^\perp$ with probability at most $\eps/8$; otherwise $K = (K^\perp)^\perp$ is the solution set of the $t$ congruences. Kitaev's construction in the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:uniform-compilation}(iii) covers the modulus $p^e$, with $p^e$ in place of $p$ (also for $p = 2$, which forces $c = 1$). Compiling the $2dt$ transforms with $\eta = \eps/(16dt)$, Lemma~\ref{lem:hybrid} on the canonical subspaces, as in Corollary~\ref{cor:approx}, changes the distribution of the samples by at most $\eps/8$. The public checks and the abort rule are those of Corollary~\ref{cor:black-box}, applied to the computed solution set $K'$: $\Phi(v) = 1$ for generators $v$ of $K'$, which gives $K' \subseteq K$ because $\phi$ is additive; $K'$ is an ideal of $F$; and $\log_p|F/K'| \le L$. As there, with exact transforms $K' \supseteq K$, so every sampling failure is caught, and a deviation $K' \subsetneq K$ can come only from the approximation error. It does not affect correctness: if $K'$ passes, $\mathcal{C} = F/K'$ is a finite Lie ring of $p$-power order and class at most $c$, and $\Phi$ induces a surjective group homomorphism $\bar\Phi : (\mathcal{C},\ast) \to G$, since $\phi(K') = 0$; everything below applies to $\mathcal{C}$, and only the counts change. The kernel step thus fails with probability at most $\eps/4$, and when it succeeds, $\mathcal{C} \cong \log G$.
+
+\emph{Chart.} Store additive subgroups of $F$ that contain $K'$, that is, additive subgroups of $\mathcal{C}$, as lattices between $p^e\mathbb{Z}^d$ and $\mathbb{Z}^d$. Hermite and Smith normal forms of integer matrices, with unimodular transformation matrices, are computable in polynomial time~\cite{KannanBachem1979}, and all entries can be kept reduced modulo $p^e$. They give sums, intersections, preimages and solution sets of linear congruences modulo $p^e$. For additive subgroups $J \subseteq S$ they give elements $u_1,\dots,u_k$ of $S$ with $S/J = \bigoplus_{i=1}^k (\mathbb{Z}/p^{a_i})\bar u_i$, $a_i \ge 1$, the coordinate map and the section $\sigma(t) = \sum_i t_iu_i$, $0 \le t_i < p^{a_i}$. If $J$ is an ideal of a Lie subring $S$, the structure constants of $S/J$ are the coordinates of the $[u_i,u_j]$, computed from the bracket table of $F$. For $S = \mathcal{C}$ and $J = 0$ this is the explicit chart of $\mathcal{C}$. Since $\phi$ is additive, $\bar\Phi(\sigma(t))$ is the Lazard sum of the powers $\Phi(u_i)^{t_i}$, at $O_c(k\,e\log p)$ multiplications once the $\Phi(u_i)$ are computed. The function $f\circ\bar\Phi$ hides $U = \bar\Phi^{-1}(H)$ in $(\mathcal{C},\ast)$, and $\mathfrak{u} = \log U$ is a Lie subring of $\mathcal{C}$.
+
+\emph{Socle recursion.} This is the normalizer recursion of~\cite[Section~3, Lemma~2]{IvanyosSanselmeSantha2012}, run through socles as in~\cite[Lemma~3]{IvanyosSanselmeSantha2012}. Let $b' = \log_p|\mathcal{C}| \le L$. Maintain Lie subrings $J \subseteq S$ of $\mathcal{C}$ with $J$ an ideal of $S$, starting with $J = 0$ and $S = \mathcal{C}$, and repeat:
+\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*),leftmargin=2em]
+  \item If $S = J$, output $J$.
+  \item Present $S/J$ as above. Its socle $\Omega = (S/J)[p]$ is an ideal of $S/J$, since $p[x,y] = [px,y]$, with $\Fp$-basis $p^{a_i-1}\bar u_i$. The coordinate on $\bar u_k$ of every element of $\Omega$ is divisible by $p^{a_k-1}$, so dividing the coordinates of $[p^{a_i-1}u_i,p^{a_j-1}u_j]$ by these powers gives $\Omega$ as an explicit $\Fp$-Lie algebra of class at most $c$, with a basis map $\iota$ into $S/J$. Run the algorithm of Theorem~\ref{thm:main} on it, with oracle $f\circ\bar\Phi\circ\sigma\circ\iota$ and error $\eps/(2b')$, which is rational, and let $V \subseteq \Omega$ be the span of its output.
+  \item If $V = 0$, or $V$ is not closed under the bracket, output $J$.
+  \item Otherwise replace $J$ by $J' = q^{-1}(V)$ and $S$ by the idealizer $I_S(J') = \{x \in S : [x,J'] \subseteq J'\}$, a solution set of linear congruences, and go to~(1).
+\end{enumerate}
+In~(4), $J'$ is a Lie subring of $S$ strictly containing $J$, and $I_S(J')$ is a Lie subring, by the Jacobi identity, in which $J'$ is an ideal. So every pair reached satisfies the hypotheses above, whatever the earlier calls returned. A call is made only when $J \ne S$, so $|J| < p^{b'}$, and $|J| \ge p^j$ after $j$ rounds; hence at most $b'$ calls are made on every branch.
+
+\emph{Validity and correctness.} Suppose $J \subseteq \mathfrak{u} \subseteq S$. Then the oracle of~(2) hides $\iota^{-1}((\mathfrak{u}/J) \cap \Omega)$ in $(\Omega,\ast)$, the subgroup $\exp\Omega$ of $(S/J,\ast)$. Indeed, for $a, a' \in \Omega$ put $g = (-\sigma(\iota a))\ast\sigma(\iota a') \in S$. Then $q(g) = (-\iota a)\ast\iota a'$, and since $\mathfrak{u}$ is a union of cosets of $J$, $g \in \mathfrak{u}$ exactly when $q(g) \in \mathfrak{u}/J$. So the call is an instance of Definition~\ref{def:input-contract}, and each of its queries is one query of $f$ and two evaluations of $\bar\Phi\circ\sigma\circ\iota$. Suppose the call returns the correct $V = (\mathfrak{u}/J)\cap\Omega$, and let $P = q^{-1}(\Omega)$, an ideal of $S$. Then $J' = \mathfrak{u}\cap P \subseteq \mathfrak{u}$, and $[x,y] \in \mathfrak{u}\cap P$ for $x \in \mathfrak{u}$ and $y \in J'$, so $\mathfrak{u} \subseteq I_S(J')$, and the invariant is preserved. If $\mathfrak{u} \ne J$, then $\mathfrak{u}/J$ is a nonzero finite $p$-group, so $V = (\mathfrak{u}/J)[p] \ne 0$ is a subalgebra and the recursion continues. If $\mathfrak{u} = J$, the output $J$ is correct, at~(1) or~(3). The invariant holds initially, so if every call is correct the output is $\mathfrak{u}$. Conditioned on the earlier calls being correct, each call is a valid instance and errs with probability at most $\eps/(2b')$, so by a union bound over the first incorrect call the recursion errs with probability at most $\eps/2$. After an incorrect call the oracle need not hide a subgroup, but by Remark~\ref{rem:oblivious} every later call still makes at most its query count and runs within its time bound, so no time-out is needed. The images under $\bar\Phi$ of additive generators of $\mathfrak{u}$ generate $H$, the image of $U$. With the kernel step, the error is at most $\eps/4 + \eps/2 < \eps$.
+
+\emph{Counts.} Call $j$ has input dimension $k_j = \operatorname{rk}(S/J) \le \log_p|S/J| \le b' \le L$ and $g_{d,j} \le k_j$. Its $\tau$ is $\lceil\log_p(6b'/\eps)\rceil \le \tau'$, and $B_d$ is increasing, so it makes at most $c(L+\tau')\prod_{d=2}^c B_d(L)$ queries, and at most $L$ calls give $Q_{\mathrm{worst}}$. On the event $K' = K$, $\mathcal{C} \cong \log G$ and $b' = b$. Then $k_j \le \operatorname{rk}S \le n$. For $d \ge 2$, $\gamma_d(\Omega)$ lies in the socle of $[S/J,S/J] = ([S,S]+J)/J$, a quotient of the subgroup $[S,S]$ of $[\log G,\log G]$, so $g_{d,j} \le R$. The other costs are, per call, the running time of Theorem~\ref{thm:main}, $\poly_c(k_j,\log p,w,\log(b'/\eps))$ by Remark~\ref{rem:oblivious}; $O_c(L\,e\log p)$ multiplications per evaluation of $\bar\Phi\circ\sigma\circ\iota$; and $\poly(d,e\log p)$ normal-form arithmetic per round, with at most $b'+1$ rounds. Here $d \le c\,s^c$ and $e\log_2 p \le \ell$.
+\end{proof}
+
+\begin{remark}[Source of the route]
+\label{rem:black-box-pe-source}
+The reduction from exponent $p^e$ to exponent $p$ is not new. Ivanyos, Sanselme and Santha reduce the HSP in any class of groups of constant nilpotency class that is closed under subgroups and quotients to $p$-groups of exponent $p$ with hidden subgroup trivial or of order $p$~\cite[Theorem~2]{IvanyosSanselmeSantha2012}: their Lemma~2 is a normalizer recursion, and their Lemma~3 passes, when $p$ exceeds the class, to the subgroup of elements of order dividing $p$ (numbering of Section~3 of the arXiv version). Their reduction is stated for groups given by refined polycyclic presentations with polynomial-time collection, which, as they note, a quantum algorithm computes for solvable black-box groups. The proof of Corollary~\ref{cor:black-box-pe} follows that route on the acquired Lazard chart. Each call recovers the whole intersection of the hidden subring with the socle of the current section, no polycyclic presentation or collection is needed, and the counts are explicit. We do not claim the route as new. A finite computation (Appendix~\ref{app:artifacts}) runs the exponent, pullback, kernel, chart and recursion steps of Corollary~\ref{cor:black-box-pe} from $40$-bit opaque handles on $48$ groups of orders $3^4$ to $3^6$, $5^4$ and $5^5$, of class $2$ to $4$ and exponent $9$ to $125$. With a brute-force oracle-only field solver in place of Theorem~\ref{thm:main}, it recovers all $5359$ hidden subgroups tested within the call cap and validates every field call as an instance of Definition~\ref{def:input-contract}; the kernel readout from the exact sampling law equals $\ker\phi$ in $958$ of $960$ trials, and the public check rejects the other two. It does not exercise Theorem~\ref{thm:main}, the Fourier approximation or any cost, and proves nothing for other groups.
+\end{remark}
+
 \paragraph{Classical query complexity.} In the same oracle model with $m \ge 2$, every classical randomized algorithm needs more than $\sqrt{2(1-\delta)\,p^{m-1}}$ queries to succeed with probability $1-\delta$, $\delta < 1/2$, even when $H$ is promised to be non-normal of order $p$ (if $\mathcal{L}$ is not abelian), and $O(p^{(m-1)/2})$ queries suffice (Proposition~\ref{prop:classical-query}). With Theorem~\ref{thm:main} this gives a query separation, whose limits Remark~\ref{rem:query-separation} records. We claim no speedup for any concrete problem, and no application of the algorithm is known.
 
 \subsection{Related Work}
 \label{sec:related}
 
-We compare with nearby results by scope only. Ivanyos, Sanselme and Santha solve the HSP in nilpotent groups of class two by twisting right actions by automorphisms, and give a classical reduction for constant class to groups of exponent $p$ with hidden subgroup of order at most $p$~\cite[Theorem~2]{IvanyosSanselmeSantha2012}. Friedl, Ivanyos, Magniez, Santha and Sen solve the HSP in solvable groups of bounded derived length and bounded exponent~\cite{FIMSS2014}; here the exponent $p$ grows. Bacon, Childs and van Dam~\cite{BaconChildsVanDam2005} solve the HSP in the groups $\Fp \ltimes \Fp^m$ with $m$ constant, for hidden subgroups of order $p$ not contained in $\Fp^m$. Ivanyos and Santha~\cite[Corollary~8]{IvanyosSantha2015} remove the restriction on $m$ when the nilpotency class is constant, by a reduction~\cite[Proposition~7]{IvanyosSantha2015} to a hidden polynomial graph problem that Decker, H{\o}yer, Ivanyos and Santha~\cite{DeckerHoyerIvanyosSantha2014} had solved for constant $m$ and degree. Once $p$ exceeds the class, these groups are Lazard groups of class below $p$ whose Lie algebra the matrix logarithm gives explicitly, and their hidden subgroups are a special case of an arbitrary $H$. So, when $p > c$, the instances treated by these results are instances of the setting of Theorem~\ref{thm:main} (for $p$ at most the class,~\cite{IvanyosSantha2015} uses~\cite{FIMSS2014}, which is not covered here); this compares scope only, we do not compare running times, and the bounds of those papers are not implied by ours. The growing-prime, fixed-class feature is not new by itself. The grade-by-grade elimination of Section~\ref{sec:descent} is also not new as a mechanism: it is the degree-by-degree elimination by scalar weights in the proof of~\cite[Theorem~5, Section~5]{IvanyosSantha2015}, transplanted to arbitrary Lazard groups through the Rees cover. What may be new is the use of the cover to supply that grading for an arbitrary hidden subgroup in an arbitrary Lazard group.
